SISSIz NEWS
===========

Unreleased
----------

Correctness

* Pairwise alignments produced a degenerate null model. The two taxon
  Newick string was written with "%.f", zero decimal places, so any
  divergence below 0.5 became a zero branch length and every sampled
  alignment was an exact copy of its ancestor. On 40 pairwise Rfam
  alignments the sampled mean pairwise identity was 1.0000 against an
  input mean of 0.7960; correcting it moves the mean z-score by -2.01
  and raises detection at z < -2 from 47.5% to 70.0%. Every two
  sequence run made with an earlier version is affected.

* Simulated time only advanced when a proposed substitution was
  accepted, which pinned the total substitution rate at one per site
  per unit branch length whatever the composition. Time now advances on
  every proposal of the uniformised jump chain. Drawing 200 alignments
  from SISSIz's own null model and scoring them, the mean z-score moves
  from +0.292 to +0.060 on an AT rich alignment; the offset means the
  released version reported a shifted false positive rate on skewed
  windows. Also makes strongly skewed input more than ten times faster.

* A composition skewed enough to zero every triplet exit rate produced
  a rate matrix of zeros, and the mutation loop never terminated. Such
  input is now refused with a message and a non-zero exit.

* Ambiguity codes were counted as adenine. The nucleotide map defaulted
  to zero, so N, R, Y and friends all fed the model an A: a fixture
  that is 12.5% N reported an A frequency of 0.6. They are now excluded
  from the model and reported on stderr.

* --nossr did nothing. Site specific rates were installed before the
  flag was tested, so the second regression used them regardless.

* A leftover debug printf wrote the maximum likelihood tree to stdout
  whenever kappa was estimated, gluing the Newick string onto the
  tab separated result line.

* The site rate adjustment divided by zero when every site exceeded the
  maximum divergence, and the singular matrix check in phyml was
  written "!Matinv(...)<0", which is always false.

Robustness

* Fixed a heap buffer overflow: MAF sequence names have no length
  limit but were copied into PHYML's 100 byte name buffer. Reachable
  from input data with --tstv, and caught by AddressSanitizer.

* The Newick buffer was a fixed 10 kB on the stack; a 400 taxon tree
  needs about 15 kB. It is now sized from the alignment.

* The rejection sampler skipped freeAln on every rejected alignment and
  retried forever, leaking about 28 MB per second when --precision was
  set tighter than the sampler could reach. Rejected samples are freed
  and the retries are capped.

* The cumulative scans in choosenuc and cchoosetriplet walked past the
  end of their probability arrays when rounding left the running sum
  short of the drawn value.

* Out of range values for --num-samples, --precision, --num-regression
  and --gamma ran to completion and reported nan, or a z-score of zero
  which reads as "not significant". They are now rejected.

Random numbers

* New --seed option. Given the same seed a run reproduces exactly.

* The default seed now comes from system entropy. time(NULL) has one
  second resolution, so screens launching many jobs at once drew
  identical sample sets.

* start_kiss seeded only x, leaving y, z and w on the same trajectory in
  every run, so different seeds produced streams that differed by
  nothing but the LCG component. All four state words are now mixed
  from the seed.

Build and testing

* Fixed compilation with modern compilers: empty parameter lists mean
  (void) in C23, and a plain inline definition emits no external symbol
  under C99 rules.

* The bundled convenience archives are linked by path, so an installed
  ViennaRNA in an earlier -L directory no longer satisfies -lRNA.

* configure appends -O2 when CFLAGS carries no -O flag. An inherited
  CFLAGS suppresses the -g -O2 that AC_PROG_CC would otherwise supply,
  which was building the whole package unoptimised and cost a factor of
  2.3 in runtime.

* New test suite: "make check" for the fast functional tests,
  "make check-asan" for a sanitizer build, "make check-valgrind" for a
  leak sweep, "make check-stats" for the null distribution check. See
  section 1.1 of the README. All of it runs on GitHub Actions.

* Plugged three leaks valgrind found. rateT2 released the pointer array
  of its scratch rate matrix but never the 64 rows behind it, losing
  32 kB per run; treeML never tore down the tree, distance matrix and
  sequence data PHYML had allocated, losing 357 kB per run with --tstv;
  and main did not release the Newick buffer or the sampled identities.
  Nine of the ten shipped invocations are now valgrind clean, the tenth
  down from 357 kB to 3.4 kB. None of these grew with --num-samples.

* The output field list in the README was missing the alignment length
  and so numbered every field after it one too low.
